# ESP32-DevKitC V4 — modeled on the ESP32-WROOM-32E variant: 4 MB flash, no
# PSRAM.
#
# This is a VARIANT CALL, not an unverified fact, and it is now sourced.
# The board is sold with nine different modules and the user guide says so
# without ever naming a memory size: "different ESP32 modules: ESP32-WROOM-32E,
# ESP32-WROOM-32UE, ESP32-WROVER-E, ESP32-WROVER-IE, ESP32-WROOM-32D,
# ESP32-WROOM-32U, ESP32-WROOM-DA (End of Life), ESP32-SOLO-1, ESP32-WROOM-32
# ... For details please refer to ESP Product Selector."
# https://docs.espressif.com/projects/esp-dev-kits/en/latest/esp32/esp32-devkitc/user_guide.html
# The only first-party memory figure for the PRODUCT is the product page's
# spec strip, "4 / 8 MB Flash":
# https://www.espressif.com/en/dev-board/esp32-devkitc-en
# ESP32-WROOM-32E/32UE Datasheet v2.1, Table 1, gives the module's own SKUs:
# N4 / N8 / N16 = 4 / 8 / 16 MB (Quad SPI), no PSRAM; only the -N*R2 SKUs add
# 2 MB (Quad SPI) PSRAM. 4 MB is the smallest and the common fit, so it is the
# safe default — an 8/16 MB board simply under-uses its flash.
# https://documentation.espressif.com/esp32-wroom-32e_esp32-wroom-32ue_datasheet_en.pdf
# WATCH OUT for the WROVER-E/IE option: those add 8 MB PSRAM and need
# CONFIG_SPIRAM=y, and their GPIO16/GPIO17 are consumed by the PSRAM. The user
# guide states it: "The pins GPIO16 and GPIO17 are available for use only on
# the boards with the modules ESP32-WROOM and ESP32-SOLO-1. The boards with
# ESP32-WROVER modules have the pins reserved for internal use."
# 40 MHz / DIO are conservative software choices; the module's published limit
# is FC 80 MHz (datasheet Tables 17/18), and QIO/DIO is an ESP-IDF setting
# that no Espressif datasheet ever states.
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_240=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_4MB=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_DIO=y
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHFREQ_40M=y
